id: pm-context-14gn
title: "YAML run-block indentation reaches shellScalars undedented, so a heredoc terminator written inside a run block never ends its payload"
description: "CodeRabbit Security/Minor on PR 84: publishInvocationsIn passes workflow text to shellScalars with the YAML block indentation attached, and the heredoc terminator is the one whitespace-sensitive rule — a real terminator never matches and later assignments stay unindexed."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 1
tags[2]: scanner,supply-chain
created_at: "2026-08-31T12:30:34.919Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-31T12:39:23.948Z"
closed_at: "2026-08-31T12:39:23.932Z"
completed_at: "2026-08-31T12:39:23.932Z"
claim_principal: pi-agent
author: pi-agent
resolution: "dedentRunBlocks learns each run: block's indentation from its first non-blank line as YAML does, strips exactly that prefix, keeps deeper indentation, ends the block at the first less-indented non-blank line, and treats only run: keys as blocks. publishInvocationsIn applies it for .github/workflows and composite-action paths, because that is where YAML hands bash dedented text; a plain shell script keeps its own indentation, and an indented terminator there is not a terminator in bash - the opposite error is guarded by the scripts/release.sh regression."
expected_result: "The run-block bypass regression must fail (one failure naming the missing flag) when the terminator is not recognised and pass after; its positive assertion (a post-terminator FLAG carries the attestation, zero failures) must fail when the terminator is not recognised; the plain-shell regression must fail if terminator matching is made whitespace-tolerant anywhere; the two dedent unit tests must fail if the dedent is removed."
actual_result: "Revert-proof by hand: reverting the dedent to identity reddened exactly the run-block bypass test and both dedent unit tests together with the heredoc tests (92 pass / 5 fail); making the terminator comparison whitespace-tolerant reddened exactly the plain-shell guard test (89 pass / 1 fail). Restored: 357/357, npm run coverage 100/100/100, npm run check, npm run docstring, npm run release:check all green, and the repository's own gate verify-release-publish-attestation passes on the real workflows. pm test --run collides with this repository's own pm-integration tests inside the linked-test sandbox (pre-existing, files untouched by this change); every linked command was executed directly and observed green, including single-title runs."
comments[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-31T12:32:59.647Z",pi-agent,"Determined by reading the path and by measurement, not assertion. publishInvocationsIn passes source.text (the raw file) through joinContinuations to shellScalars — no dedent anywhere. Pattern audit over every whitespace-sensitive rule: STANDALONE_ASSIGNMENT opens with ^[ \\t]* and tolerates the indentation; control closers and function openers are matched against code.trim(); a comment starts after any of [\\s;&|(){}] so leading whitespace qualifies; bashArrays anchors on (?:^|\\s); heredoc START detection is a character scan. Exactly ONE rule is defeated: the heredoc terminator comparison (candidate === delimiter on the whole line). Inside a run: | block a real terminator therefore never matches, the heredoc swallows the rest of the file, every later assignment is payload, and a $NPM publish after it is omitted while an attested sibling satisfies the non-vacuity guard. Measured end to end: the realistic run-block fixture returned failures: 0 before the fix (clean over an unattested publish) and failures: 1 after; the same text hand-dedented always returned 1.\n\nFix: dedentRunBlocks(text), applied in publishInvocationsIn for files matching the workflow/action YAML paths only. It learns each run: block's indentation from its first non-blank line (as YAML does), strips exactly that prefix, keeps deeper indentation, ends the block at the first non-blank line indented less, and only run: keys are treated as blocks (a block scalar under any other key is data no shell runs). A plain shell script is deliberately NOT dedented: its indentation is the shell's own, and bash does not accept an indented terminator without <<- — so a whitespace-tolerant terminator comparison or an ungated dedent would index payload prose as a scalar (the opposite error), which the scripts/release.sh regression rejects."
